From f7b89af61cfa245419db4a47ce18e864b975fb14 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JorreFog <JorreFog@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 19:40:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ARMJIT_A64: fewer host instructions per block and per exit

Each change is on by default and exact (same cycles, memory and pictures). MELONDS_JIT_OLDCODEGEN=1
turns all of them off (the code is then what patches 0001-0006 generate); MELONDS_JIT_NO<name>=1
turns off one. All are read once, when the compiler is created.

- FASTENTRY: the fast memory base (x26) is set by a four-instruction prologue at each block's
  entry, which the runtime dispatches to; link slots and DynExit enter past it (x26 is callee-saved
  and only blocks of the same CPU set it), so a linked block doesn't reload it.
- CONDEXIT: a conditional branch with a constant target ending a block gets a known exit each way
  (ExitCheck and a link slot), instead of DynExit's lookup; the condition is checked again on the
  host's flags or RCPSR.
- MEMADDR: a 16/32-bit [rn] fast access uses rn as the address instead of a copy in w0; a patched
  (faulted) access moves it to w0 before its call (LoadStorePatch::AddrReg).
- CODESTORES: ARM7 CodeRegion/CodeCycles in one 64-bit store; a constant jump skips storing
  CodeRegion/CodeCycles (ARM7) or RegionCodeCycles (ARM9) when the block already stored the same
  values on every path to it.
- DEAD: no CLS/CLZ before ARM7 multiplies (Comp_AddCycles_CI ignores the register), no ARM9
  CodeCycles store before compiled instructions (only the interpreter reads it), no R15 load into a
  host register for a conditional B/BL.
- EXITSTUB: ExitCheck/DynExit add the cycles with one sxtw-extended add, return to ARM_Ret through a
  literal, and come in a variant without the CPSR store for blocks that left RCPSR as it is in
  memory; DynExit computes the lookup address in fewer instructions and loads the code base from a
  literal.
- EXITPC: a constant exit's R15 is stored by the stub (ExitCheckPC, from a literal word after the
  link slot) instead of a mov/movk/str in the block.

MELONDS_JIT_PERFMAP also names the shared stubs in the map (stub_e9xx/e7xx JumpTo, ec0n ExitCheck,
ed0n DynExit, eb00 the rest), and MELONDS_JIT_PERFDIR puts the map and code dump elsewhere than /tmp.
